{"causes": ["The ongoing Anglo-French Wars created a constant state of naval tension and conflict in the early 16th century.", "The strategic importance of Brittany's coastline and its recent integration into the French kingdom necessitated a strong naval defense.", "The development of powerful carracks and advanced artillery transformed naval warfare into engagements of immense destructive potential."], "effects": ["Hervé de Portzmoguer became a potent symbol of French and Breton naval heroism, inspiring future generations of mariners.", "The dramatic mutual destruction of the *Cordelière* and *Regent* at Saint-Mathieu intensified Anglo-French naval rivalry.", "The name "Primauguet" was adopted by several ships of the French Navy, perpetuating Portzmoguer's legacy of valor."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "hervé-de-portzmoguer", "sourceName": "Hervé de Portzmoguer", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-saint-mathieu", "targetName": "Battle of Saint-Mathieu", "context": "Hervé de Portzmoguer famously engaged in the Battle of Saint-Mathieu on August 10, 1512, where his ship was destroyed."}, {"sourceSlug": "anne-of-brittany", "sourceName": "Anne of Brittany", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "hervé-de-portzmoguer", "targetName": "Hervé de Portzmoguer", "context": "As Duchess of Brittany, Anne of Brittany's efforts to maintain Breton autonomy and alliance with France influenced naval officers like Portzmoguer's service."}, {"sourceSlug": "hervé-de-portzmoguer", "sourceName": "Hervé de Portzmoguer", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "french-naval-heroism", "targetName": "French Naval Heroism", "context": "Portzmoguer's self-sacrificial action at Saint-Mathieu established a powerful archetype for French naval heroism."}, {"sourceSlug": "hervé-de-portzmoguer", "sourceName": "Hervé de Portzmoguer", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "breton-coast", "targetName": "Breton Coast", "context": "The decisive naval engagement involving Hervé de Portzmoguer, the Battle of Saint-Mathieu, occurred off the coast of Brittany in 1512."}, {"sourceSlug": "hervé-de-portzmoguer", "sourceName": "Hervé de Portzmoguer", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "louis-xii-of-france", "targetName": "Louis XII of France", "context": "Hervé de Portzmoguer served as a naval officer under King Louis XII of France during the Italian Wars, specifically against England."}], "places": [{"name": "Pointe Saint-Mathieu, France", "role": "Site of last battle"}, {"name": "Brest, France", "role": "Home port"}, {"name": "Brittany, France", "role": "Region of origin and conflict"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Hervé de Portzmoguer", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "hervé-de-portzmoguer"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q555050", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herv%C3%A9_de_Portzmoguer"], "tags": ["Naval Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Hervé de Portzmoguer's legendary sacrifice at the Battle of Saint-Mathieu created a foundational myth for French naval heroism and contributed to the consolidation of Breton identity within the French kingdom. His dramatic death, leading to the destruction of two major warships and over a thousand lives, stands as a pivotal moment in early 16th-century naval warfare.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-25T03:14:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45538261205", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Hervé de Portzmoguer, also known as "Primauguet", was a Breton naval officer who was killed in action when his ship blew up at the Battle of Saint-Mathieu.", "newValue": "Hervé de Portzmoguer, often remembered by his ship's name "Primauguet," was a distinguished Breton naval officer whose heroic, self-sacrificial death in 1512 cemented his place as a national symbol of maritime courage and loyalty. Operating during a pivotal era of Anglo-French rivalry and the complex integration of Brittany into the Kingdom of France, Portzmoguer served as captain of the carrack *Marie-la-Cordelière*, a flagship of the combined Franco-Breton fleet. His final engagement, the Battle of Saint-Mathieu, off the coast of Brittany, became a legendary episode in naval history, epitomizing the fierce and often brutal nature of early modern maritime conflict.

On August 10, 1512, during the War of the League of Cambrai, Portzmoguer's *Cordelière* engaged the much larger English flagship, the *Regent*, commanded by Sir Thomas Knyvett. In a desperate and prolonged close-quarters battle, the two mighty carracks became entangled. With both ships severely damaged and ablaze, Portzmoguer, refusing to surrender and facing imminent defeat, reportedly ordered his powder magazine to be ignited. The ensuing cataclysmic explosion obliterated both the *Cordelière* and the *Regent*, killing nearly all 1,500 men aboard the two vessels, including both captains, in one of the most destructive naval encounters of its time.

Portzmoguer's dramatic demise transformed him into an enduring symbol of Breton and, subsequently, French naval heroism. His act of defiant self-destruction resonated deeply, representing the indomitable spirit of sailors defending their homeland against overwhelming odds. The narrative of his sacrifice served to unify the recently integrated Duchy of Brittany with the French Crown, providing a shared legend of valor that transcended regional identities. His legacy continues to inspire, with several ships of the French Navy, including a modern frigate, bearing the name *Primauguet* in his honor."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-25T03:14:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45538261205", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1470, Plouarzel"}, {"title": "Naval warfare and maritime strategy", "type": "Idea", "year": "1470, Plouarzel"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Anglo-French Wars created a constant state of naval tension and conflict in the early 16th century.", "The strategic importance of Brittany's coastline and its recent integration into the French kingdom necessitated a strong naval defense.", "The development of powerful carracks and advanced artillery transformed naval warfare into engagements of immense destructive potential."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-25T03:14:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45538261205", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Influenced naval strategy and maritime power dynamics", "type": "Idea", "year": "1512-08-20"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1512-08-20"}], "newValue": ["Hervé de Portzmoguer became a potent symbol of French and Breton naval heroism, inspiring future generations of mariners.", "The dramatic mutual destruction of the *Cordelière* and *Regen